[00:57:08.980 --> 00:57:38.960]   Um, sir, there's a, you know, we, we've got, uh, people process and technology, right, so all those things are, um, are levered to help, you know, our capital program, as well as our, our operating program, so, um, you know, we continue to, uh, implement, uh, systems and technology that, um, improves processes that makes it more, affect, you know, efficient and effective workforce, so, we have, um, you know, a
[00:57:38.960 --> 00:58:08.940]   still a robust, I'd say, series of, uh, technology improvements, um, if you, if you, uh, I'll start off by just setting the space, in the guidance we gave, we said that we expected O&M costs to be, you know, down this year, and then just for the forecast period, kind of, uh, last, uh, going, going forward, so, how, how are we able to do that, um, it is by some of these technology changes, and we've been implementing more
[00:58:08.940 --> 00:58:38.920]   productivity management tools, and tools for our, you know, individual line workers, and gasfitters, and, in the field, to get their work, to update their, um, work, that we can then take that, and automatically update drawings and files, we don't need, you know, to hand it off to somebody, and, so, there's still, um, these productivity, uh, technology changes that are happening, some went in last year, some going in this year, and more, uh, plan for next, so, that's, um,
[00:58:38.920 --> 00:58:51.360]   That will be, I'd say, the lever, the underpinning for us to have the ability to continue to improve processes and take unneeded costs out of the business.
[00:58:51.360 --> 00:58:55.800]   Okay, great. That's helpful. Thanks so much for the call.
